# How do you make a plan in the middlegame?
teaches: ["strategy/planning","strategy/king-attack"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "How do you find the weakness to aim at? When should you attack the king, and how do pawn storms work when kings castle on opposite sides?"
next: {"title":"Week 3 review: openings and plans","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/22-week-3-review-openings-and-plans.cast"}

## Warm-up: pawns and outposts
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 6k1/ppp5/8/PPP5/8/8/8/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [b6, axb6, c6, bxc6, a6]}
      - {fen: 5rk1/p1q1rppp/1p1p4/4p3/4P3/3BN3/1PPQ1PPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nd5, Qd8, Nxe7+, Qxe7], highlights: [d5]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: how to make a plan when nothing is hanging. First, two warm-ups from last week: pawns, and outposts.
    draw position

B: Three pawns against three, and both kings far away. Nobody gets through.
    highlight piece_a5 piece_b5 piece_c5

A: Look again. His king is too far away to stop a passed pawn.
    highlight piece_g8

    pause 0.3

    ask "Make a queen." widget chess line true hints ["Give a pawn away first.","Push the middle pawn."] themes ["pawn structure"] voice a teaches ["strategy/pawn-structure"]
        right: Pawn to b6: the a-pawn takes, pawn to c6, the b-pawn takes, and your a-pawn runs. His king is too far to catch it.
        wrong: Push the middle pawn first, then see what each capture leaves open.

B: Two pawns thrown away, and the third one walks in.
    highlight piece_a6

A: Next: a quiet middlegame, White to move.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: The hole on d5. No black pawn can ever chase a piece from there.
    draw hl_d5

B: I'd put my knight on it one day.
    point at.ref piece_e3 gesture circle

A: Why one day? Look what the knight would hit from there.
    highlight piece_e3

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["The knight on e3.","What would it attack from d5?"] themes ["outpost","fork"] voice a teaches ["strategy/piece-activity","tactics/fork"]
        right: Knight to d5 hits the queen and the rook. The queen steps to d8 to guard the rook, and the knight takes it anyway, with check: a rook for a knight.
        wrong: Put the knight on the hole, and look at what it attacks.

B: So the outpost wasn't just a nice square. It was where the knight did its work.
    point at.ref sq_d5 gesture circle

A: A weak square or a weak pawn is where a plan starts.
    animate plies_shown 0 duration 1.2

A: Today: how to find it, and when to go for the king instead.
    point at.ref sq_d5 gesture circle

## A target, then a count
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 2rr2k1/p3qppp/1p2bn2/3p4/8/1QN1PB2/PP3PPP/3R1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxd5, Nxd5, Bxd5, Bxd5, Rxd5, Rxd5, Qxd5], arrows: [{from: g2, to: g4}, {from: h2, to: h4}]}
      - {fen: r4rk1/ppqb1ppp/1np1p3/3pP1N1/3P1P2/2PB4/PP4PP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxh7+, Kh8, Qh5, Be8, Bg6+, Kg8, Qh7#]}
      - {fen: 2r2rk1/pp2ppbp/3p1np1/q7/4P1PP/2N1BP2/PPPQ4/1K1R3R w - - 0 1, arrows: [{from: g4, to: g5}, {from: h4, to: h5}, {from: b7, to: b4}]}
      - {fen: 2r2rk1/pp2ppb1/3p2p1/q5N1/4P2R/2N2P2/PPP4Q/1K6 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rh8+, Bxh8, Qh7#]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
    counts:
      - d5
sources:
    - id: steinitz
      title: The Theory of Steinitz (after D. Hooper, British Chess Magazine, September 1984)
      authors: Exeter Chess Club
      year: 2010
      finding: 'In the theory credited to Steinitz: an attack cannot succeed against correct defence while the position is in equilibrium; a player should attack only with an advantage; Lasker added that the attack goes against the weakest spot.'
      url: https://exeterchessclub.org.uk/node/606
    - id: fischer
      title: Slaying the dragon (Fischer–Larsen, Portorož 1958)
      authors: Oliver Reeh (ChessBase)
      year: 2020
      finding: 'In his notes to Fischer–Larsen in My 60 Memorable Games, Fischer summed up White''s attack against the Dragon: pry open the h-file (the KR-file), sac, sac... mate.'
      url: https://en.chessbase.com/post/tactic-blog-oliver-reeh-2020_34_e

    card title "A target, then a count"

A: Now the lesson. No piece is loose, and he threatens nothing. So what's the plan?
    draw position

B: Go for his king. My g-pawn and h-pawn march up the board.
    draw arrow_0 arrow_1 parallel true

A: His king has three pawns at home and a knight beside it: nothing to bite on.
    highlight piece_f6 piece_f7 piece_g7 piece_h7

    erase arrow_0 arrow_1

A: And with both kings on this side, those pawns are your own king's roof.
    highlight piece_f2 piece_g2 piece_h2

A: An old idea, credited to the first world champion, Wilhelm Steinitz: a sound position can't be beaten by attack.
    portrait steinitz cites ["steinitz"] x 912 y 560 width 130 of "Wilhelm Steinitz" cameo true

A: You aim at a weakness. Which black pawn is the weak one?
    highlight piece_d5 piece_b6 piece_h7

    erase steinitz

    quiz "Which black pawn is the weak one?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 962 buttons_at.y 375 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: d5: no c- or e-pawn can ever guard it.
        wrong: Which pawn has no pawn beside it to guard it?
        + d5
        * b6
        * h7

B: Then I count. Four of mine hit it, and three of his guard it.
    draw count_d5

A: So the trades go: knights, bishops, rooks,
    animate plies_shown 6 duration 3.5

A: and whatever order he takes in, the last capture is yours. A pawn won, and his king never came into it.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 7 duration 1.3

    pause 2.2

    highlight piece_d5

    erase count_d5

A: So when is the king the target? A new position, White to move.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: His knight has wandered off to b6. I'd chase it with my a-pawn.
    highlight piece_b6

A: Before you chase it, count around his king. Your bishop, knight and queen are close; his knight has left, and no bishop guards g5.
    highlight piece_d3 piece_g5 piece_d1

A: And the centre is locked, so he can't hit back in the middle. Chase the knight, or attack?
    highlight piece_d4 piece_e5 piece_d5 piece_e6

    quiz "Chase the knight with a4, or attack his king?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 962 buttons_at.y 375 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: His king: three of your pieces are near it, only pawns and a rook defend it, and the locked centre gives him no counterplay.
        wrong: Count the pieces near his king: yours, then his.
        * Pawn to a4
        + His king

    pause 0.3

    ask "Attack." widget chess line 1 hints ["Checks first.","Your bishop on d3."] themes ["king attack","combination"] voice a teaches ["strategy/king-attack","calculation/combination"]
        right: Bishop takes h7, check. The knight guards h7, so the king can't take: he must hide on h8.
        wrong: Which check near his king can he not answer by taking?

A: Then queen to h5. Whatever he plays, the bishop steps aside with check, and queen to h7 is mate.
    animate plies_shown 7 duration 4.5

A: That's a test, not a law: more attackers than defenders near the king, and a centre that can't open. Then go for the king.
    highlight piece_h7 piece_g5

A: Last case: the kings castled on opposite sides. Yours is on b1, his on g8.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Then I'd make my king safe first. Pawn to a3.
    highlight piece_a2

    draw arrow_0 arrow_1 parallel true

A: Here your pawns can run at his king, because your own king isn't behind them. That's a pawn storm.
    blocking false

    pause 3

    highlight piece_g4 piece_h4

    draw arrow_2

A: His pawns run at yours, so it's a race. And a3 even gives his b-pawn a target. So whose storm hits something first?
    blocking false

    pause 2.6

    highlight piece_a2

    pause 2.4

    highlight arrow_0 arrow_1 arrow_2

    quiz "Whose pawn storm hits something first?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 962 buttons_at.y 375 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: White: h5 hits g6 next move; his b-pawn needs two.
        wrong: Count the moves until each pawn touches something.
        + White
        * Black
        * Level

A: That g6 pawn is a hook: a pawn that has already moved gives yours something to hit.
    highlight piece_g6

    erase arrow_0 arrow_1 arrow_2

A: A few moves later in a game like this, the h-pawns are gone and the file is open. You're behind in material, but his king has no cover.
    blocking false

    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    pause 2.8

    highlight piece_h4 piece_h2

    pause 2.6

    highlight piece_g8

A: Bobby Fischer's recipe against this set-up: pry open the h-file, sac, sac, mate.
    portrait fischer cites ["fischer"] x 912 y 540 width 130 of "Bobby Fischer" cameo true

    erase fischer

    pause 0.3

    ask "Finish it." widget chess line true hints ["Give up a rook, with check.","The rook on h4."] themes ["king attack","mating pattern"] voice a teaches ["strategy/king-attack","tactics/mating-patterns"]
        right: Rook to h8, check: the queen behind it guards h8, so the king can't take, and the bishop must. Then queen to h7, mate, guarded by the knight.
        wrong: Which check can only be answered one way?

A: So: find the weakness, count who's stronger there, and go there. The king is the target only when the count says so.
    blocking false

    animate position_shown 0 plies_shown 7 duration 0.6

    pause 3.6

    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 7 duration 0.6

## Your turn: a plan and three attacks
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Four for four. His king never had a chance.
    good: Most of them. Count around the king, and the rest will come.
    poor: King attacks hide behind a sacrifice. Count first, then look again.
    none: The kings will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 2rq1rk1/4bppp/p4n2/2p5/8/3NB3/PP2QPPP/2RR2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxc5]}
      - {fen: 1r3rk1/3bpp1p/p5p1/4q3/6P1/4BP2/P1PQ3P/1K1R3R b - - 0 1, moves: [Qb2#], flip: true}
      - {fen: r4rk1/pp3p1p/2nqp1pQ/8/2P5/8/PB3PPP/R4RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qg7#]}
      - {fen: r5rk/pp4pp/8/q2p1P1Q/3P4/2P2R2/PP4PP/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qxh7+, Kxh7, Rh3#]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now you've seen how a plan finds its target: your turn. Four positions, scored.
    draw position

A: First, a quiet one. His king has a knight, a bishop and three pawns at home. Attack it, or win the c5 pawn?
    highlight piece_f6 piece_e7

    quiz "Attack his king, or win the c5 pawn?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 962 buttons_at.y 375 buttons_layout column voice a test d21
        right: The c5 pawn: your knight, bishop and rook hit it, and only his bishop and rook guard it. His king is sound.
        wrong: Count around his king, then count on c5.
        * His king
        + The c5 pawn

A: Knight takes c5. Three against two, so taking back only loses him more.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Next, you're Black, so the board turns round. The kings castled on opposite sides.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in one." widget chess line true hints ["An open file points at his king.","Your queen."] themes ["king attack","mating pattern"] voice a test d21 teaches ["strategy/king-attack","tactics/mating-patterns"]
        right: Queen to b2, mate: the rook on b8 guards her, down the open b-file.
        wrong: Which square next to his king does your rook already cover?

B: The open b-file and the long diagonal did the work.
    highlight piece_b8

A: White again. His dark-squared bishop is gone.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

B: There must be a hidden trick here.
    highlight piece_c6 piece_d6

A: Don't hunt for tricks. Run the three questions.
    highlight piece_g8

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in one." widget chess line true hints ["Checks first.","Your queen, with the bishop behind."] themes ["king attack","mating pattern"] voice a test d21 teaches ["strategy/king-attack","tactics/mating-patterns"]
        right: Queen to g7, mate: the bishop on b2 guards her down the long diagonal.
        wrong: Which check can his king not take back?

A: Last one. Black is ahead in material, so only an attack will do.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in two." widget chess line true hints ["Give up the queen, with check.","Then the rook on f3."] themes ["king attack","mating pattern"] voice a test d21 teaches ["strategy/king-attack","tactics/mating-patterns"]
        right: Queen takes h7, check: the king must take, and the rook swings to h3, mate. The pawn on f5 covers g6.
        wrong: Which sacrifice drags his king onto an open file?

    erase position

    text score_big "Day 21 score" x 500 y 400 font_size 110

A: You scored {d21.score} of {d21.total}.
    results test d21

B: Each one started with a count.
    highlight score_big

    erase score_big

A: So a plan is a target and a count.
    text syn_target "a target" x 500 y 470 font_size 72

A: Find the weakness, bring more pieces there than he has, and only then strike.
    text syn_count "a count" x 500 y 360 font_size 72

    erase syn_target syn_count

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower. Then find its first mistake.
    text hw_game "one slow game: 15 + 10" x 500 y 440 font_size 56
    text hw_mistake "find mistake 1" x 500 y 350 font_size 56

## Where next
end_page: true

